// A SCHEMA TRANSFERRED THROUGH A MODEL — the regression guard for the two-space args bug.
//
// Two theorems over ONE model, differing ONLY in whether the cited theorem takes a predicate
// parameter. CASE A (plain) always worked. CASE B (the schema, at a predicate of the consumer's
// own) did not, and the question "a plain theorem transfers, so why not a schema?" is what this
// pair was built to answer.
//
// THE BUG (fixed): a schema instance runs passes in BOTH spaces — its statement in TARGET terms,
// a source-space pass over its body. The source pass elaborates the schema's own binders
// UN-remapped (`Elem`, not `Thing`), so the arguments it substitutes must record SOURCE sorts
// too. The no-discharge transfer path bound the args once and used that one map for both, so a
// generator param's `arg_sorts` said `Thing` while the body's binder said `Elem`, and
// `applyGeneratorParam` rejected the application INSIDE THE SOURCE FILE
// ("expected sort 'Thing', got 'Elem'") — a confusing place for a consumer's mistake to land.
//
// Note what the twin canNOT be: a re-read of the arg from a source-space AST (`sourceExpr`).
// The author writes the lambda ONCE, in target terms, and `source_ast` is populated only for a
// proof that is itself a transfer — so the twin reuses the bound body and restates only the
// recorded SORTS on the source side.
//
// What a citer gets is the source schema's own proof re-checked under the interpretation:
// `--axioms` on the equivalent probe reports NO axioms, and a wrong claim is rejected against
// the transferred statement.
//
// STILL OPEN (separate fixture needed): an ACCELERANT inside a transferred schema body — e.g.
// `[using specialize all(y)]` — runs its own source-space pass and fails the same way. See
// CONTAINER-THEORY.md.
import src <<< "model_schema_transfer_source.b4m"
sort Thing
const ROOT: Thing
pred flagged(x: Thing)
axiom rootIsFlagged: flagged(ROOT)

model ThingElem {
  src.Elem: Thing
  src.SEED: ROOT
  src.marks: flagged
  src.seedIsMarked <- rootIsFlagged
}

// CASE A — PLAIN theorem through the model. Expected: works today.
theorem somethingFlagged: exists x: Thing; flagged(x)
proof
  @conclusion |
    exists x: Thing; flagged(x)
    [using model(ThingElem) src.somethingIsMarked]
qed

// CASE B — the SCHEMA through the same model, at a predicate of the consumer's own. Same
// source file, same model, same axiom discharge. The ONLY difference from case A is that the
// cited theorem takes a predicate parameter.
theorem somethingFlaggedViaSchema: flagged(ROOT) -> exists x: Thing; flagged(x)
proof
  @conclusion |
    flagged(ROOT) -> exists x: Thing; flagged(x)
    [using model(ThingElem) src.somethingHolds((fun y: Thing => flagged(y)))]
qed
